# Contact form email delivery (EmailJS)
#
# Production values are already committed in .env.production (safe — see the
# note in that file for why). This .env.local.example is only for local dev
# if you ever want to point your local build at a *different* EmailJS
# service/template than production (e.g. a test inbox) without touching the
# committed file — copy it to .env.local (gitignored) and it'll override
# .env.production for local builds only.
#
# This site is statically exported (output: 'export' in next.config.ts) and
# deployed to GitHub Pages, so there's no server to run SMTP code directly —
# SMTP credentials can never live safely in client-side JS anyway. EmailJS
# relays the send on their end instead: you connect your own inbox to it
# once, and the contact form calls their API with a public (safe-to-expose)
# key.
#
# Setup (~5 minutes, free tier covers 200 emails/month):
#   1. Create an account at https://www.emailjs.com
#   2. Email Services -> Add New Service -> connect Gmail (or Outlook /
#      any SMTP account you own) -> note the Service ID
#   3. Email Templates -> Create New Template. Use these variables so they
#      match the form's field names: {{name}} {{email}} {{subject}}
#      {{message}} -> set "To Email" on the template to the inbox you want
#      submissions delivered to (e.g. shahzaib.warraich@turon.ai) ->
#      note the Template ID
#   4. Account -> General -> copy your Public Key
#   5. Copy this file to .env.local (already gitignored) and fill in the
#      three values below, then rebuild.
#
# These are NEXT_PUBLIC_ vars because this is a static export — they get
# baked into the client bundle at build time. That's expected and safe:
# EmailJS's public key is meant to be exposed (it only authorizes sends
# through the specific service/template you configured, with optional
# domain restrictions you can set in the EmailJS dashboard under
# Account -> Security).

NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY=
